## V-21 · T44 — sufficient, and I proved it on my own mutation

**Your question — is "no other path *can* emit it" + happy-path behaviour enough? Yes, and the
three pieces interlock rather than overlap:**

- behavioural → the emission point **is reached** on success
- structural → **no other location** in the source can emit the string
- mutation B → it **cannot be moved earlier**

Compose them: sentinel present ⟹ that one point was reached ⟹ `runMigrations` returned. **Neither
leg alone carries it** — structural alone permits a sentinel nothing reaches; behavioural alone
permitted *exactly* mutation C. That's why C survived until the structural test existed.

I re-ran mutation C myself under the full three-leg discipline (baseline proved == HEAD blob
`c6c5ea31…`; mutation inserted into `main()`'s catch; restore by absolute path, `cp` exit checked,
re-hashed against HEAD). **It fails, naming line 854.**

**The `deploy.yml` edit is correct** — `pipefail` is the load-bearing part (without it `tee`'s
exit 0 masks a migrator failure, swapping one silent-success defect for another); the
`DATABASE_URL` guard is preserved; `yaml.safe_load` parses, 7 jobs intact.

**One fragile adjacency, not a defect.** The step's own `::error::` message **contains the literal
string `MIGRATE_RUNNER_COMPLETE`**. It's safe only because the grep reads `/tmp/migrate.log` and
the echo lands in the step log afterwards. Had the grep been written against the step's own output
stream, **the failure message would satisfy the check it reports the failure of.** Worth a comment
so a later edit doesn't casually convert it into a pipeline grep.

**My V-3 RES-1 is closed by this.** Recorded, so it isn't carried forward against a landed fix.

## V-20 · T43 — the finding is right, and it names the third leg

A mutation proof has **three** legs: (1) baseline provenance, (2) mutation detection, (3) restore
fidelity. **The test proves only leg 2.** M0-T38 and V-16 closed leg 1. T43 names leg 3 — the one
where failure is *silent*, because the test's own result stays valid after a failed `cp`.

**One correction:** leg 3 is undetectable *by the test*, not undetectable. Checking the restore's
exit code and re-hashing against the HEAD blob both catch it — T43 caught its own near-miss exactly
that way. Its phrasing risks reading as "undetectable in principle," which is a counsel of despair
rather than a fixable gap. **Recommend one line added to D-40: check the restore command's exit
code and re-hash against HEAD; absolute paths only, never relative after a `cd`.**

**Live-victim test, tree-wide:** `git status` shows 3 modified tracked files — two of my own
ledgers and `nirmana_tracker.html`. **No source file is left mutated anywhere**, and
`git grep 'MUTATION [A-Z][0-9]'` over all tracked `.ts/.tsx/.py` returns nothing. Every mutation
proof I've accepted is clean on leg 3 *as measured now* — an end-state check, not a proof no
restore ever silently failed and was later fixed.

## V-18 · T36 — and the collision resolves in my favour, on better grounds than offered

**F-T36-5 is a real collision** — §8.6 stage 2 (line 883) does name "`has_substeps` corrected" as
per-rung work, which read as a rung assignment would make the 12-row repair **I certified at V-9**
an I13 breach.

**It doesn't, and the resolution isn't D-30's verification claim — it's in the plan.** §14.1's M0
row (line 1050) names *"layer-position, kind, and `has_substeps` repair"* as M0 content
**explicitly**. A specific roadmap assignment governs a generic method-section recipe. T36 was
right that the forcing wasn't written where it looked; it's written one section over.

**F-T36-4 confirmed on my own arithmetic.** At V-11 I measured UNEXAMINED = 11. In v1.1 it's 6.
The set difference is exactly `{C-04, C-06, C-07, C-15, C-17}` — **5 rules, 0 criteria**, against
the claimed "6 rules and 2 criteria."

**The headline holds, from a third source:** `gh run list --workflow=nirmana-m0-guards.yml` returns
**HTTP 404, "workflow not found on the default branch"** — GitHub's own API, not a local git read.
And the guards are honestly non-blocking (`continue-on-error: true` on the jobs, scripts keeping
exit 1), which is the H3-compliant form.

